  urldate = {2026-07-13},
  abstract = {Purpose of review               The human gut harbors a diverse community of microorganisms known as the gut microbiota. Extensive research in recent years has shed light on the profound influence of the gut microbiome on human health and disease. This review aims to explore the role of the gut microbiome in various clinical conditions and highlight the emerging therapeutic potential of targeting the gut microbiota for disease management.                                         Recent findings                                Knowledge of the influence of gut microbiota on human physiology led to the development of various therapeutic possibilities such as fecal microbiota transplant (FMT), phage therapy, prebiotics, and probiotics. Recently, the U.S. FDA approved two FMT products for the treatment of recurrent                 Clostridioides difficile                 infection with ongoing research for the treatment of various disease conditions.                                                        Summary               Advancement in the knowledge of the association between gut microbiota and various disease processes has paved the way for novel therapeutics.},
